(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "adr-2608231200-kagi-local-vault-window-is-one-document-with-no-script")
  (:adr/title "kagi's local vault window is one server-rendered document with no script")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-08-23")
  (:adr/last-verified "2026-08-23")
  (:adr/authoritative true)
  (:adr/deciders (vector "Jun Kawasaki"))
  (:adr/topic "kagi-vault-surfaces")
  (:adr/authoritative-for
  (vector "kagi ui: the local vault window"
   "zero-JavaScript local surfaces next to live credentials"
   "Referrer-Policy vs the Origin header a form POST gate needs"))
  (:adr/related
  (vector "90-docs/adr/2606272330-kagi-clj-pqc-vault.edn"
   "90-docs/adr/2607230100-kagi-v050-architecture-closure.edn"
   "90-docs/adr/2608080100-kotoba-lang-ui-is-a-single-page-app.edn"
   "90-docs/adr/2608136000-five-questions-before-believing-a-check.edn"
   "90-docs/adr/2608201300-jvm-new-surface-scaffold-guard.edn"
   "orgs/kotoba-lang/kagi/src/kagi/ui.cljc"
   "orgs/kotoba-lang/kagi/src/kagi/ui/server.cljc"
   "orgs/kotoba-lang/kagi/src/kagi/ui/actions.cljc"))
  (:adr/body "Context

`kagi` is this workspace's 1Password replacement, and until now it was a CLI
and nothing else. Everything a vault does it already did — the one thing a
terminal is worse at than a window is a LIST. `kagi.vault-read` had already
written down why that list is safe to draw: enumerating your own items is
what a password manager's list screen is, and it stays safe because listing
does not decrypt. ADR-2607230100 recorded \"GUI device grant/revoke\" as a
follow-up that was never a closure blocker, so the gap was known and named.

The repo-wide UI rules point in two directions here. ADR-2608080100 makes
the single-page app the kotoba-lang default: one document, one bundle, one
mount, views as data, fragment routing. The base design system is jp-go-dds
(owner decision 2026-08-05). Both apply. But the bundle does not: this page
is served to a browser sitting next to a process that is holding an open
vault, and a ClojureScript bundle is a large amount of code that a reviewer
of a credential surface would have to read, or trust.

Decision

`kagi ui` serves ONE server-rendered document from 127.0.0.1, on jp-go-dds,
with no JavaScript at all, and `Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'`
saying so. Views are data (`kagi.ui/views`); the nav, the routing CSS and
the redirect targets are all generated from that vector, so a view cannot
exist without being reachable. Addressing is by fragment, and the stylesheet
resolves \"no fragment\" the same way a router resolves an unknown one:
`body:not(:has(.kagi-view:target))` shows the default.

This is a scoped exception to ADR-2608080100, and the scope is the reason:
LOCAL surfaces that sit next to live credentials. What is given up is the
mount — an action posts a form and the document is drawn again, which over a
loopback socket is milliseconds. What is kept is every invariant that rule
exists to protect: one document, one shell, one stylesheet, no second
bundle to drift onto a second design system, no view that looks live and is
dead. Public marketing surfaces and full applications stay on the SPA rule.

Three properties hold the surface together.

1. The plaintext never reaches the browser. Copy reveals through the same
   `kagi.operation` graph as `kagi get` — so the AccessGovernor sees it and
   the hash-chained ledger records it, with purpose `:ui-copy` — and the
   value goes from the JVM to the clipboard. It is not written into the page
   and does not cross the socket. The browser is the largest attack surface
   on the machine and is never handed a secret.

2. Three independent gates, each refusing by its own name. The socket is
   127.0.0.1 (an absence of route, not a check). A session cookie, obtained
   by exchanging a one-time `?token=` for an HttpOnly SameSite=Strict cookie
   and an immediate redirect to a bare `/`, so the token is not left in the
   address bar or the history. And the same token again in the body of every
   POST: the cookie proves the browser is the operator's, the form token
   proves the page is ours. Refusals answer `no-session` / `bad-origin` /
   `bad-form-token`, because a guard that returns one 403 for three failures
   can be broken in one of them and go on passing its own tests
   (ADR-2608136000, question 6).

3. The server holds no vault. `kagi.ui.server` is handed three functions
   (`kagi.ui.actions`) and binds a socket; `kagi.cli` opens the vault once,
   where unlock already knows how to prompt. Revocation asks before it acts:
   the list control is a link that changes nothing, and only the second
   control is a POST.

Measured, and the reason this ADR exists rather than a comment

The page first went out with `Referrer-Policy: no-referrer`. A browser
served that header sends `Origin: null` on its OWN same-origin form POSTs —
so every Copy and every Revoke came back `bad-origin`, while the whole test
suite stayed green, because an HTTP client sends whatever Origin the test
hands it. Found by clicking the button in a real browser; confirmed against
an echo server (`no-referrer` -> `Origin: \"null\"`, `same-origin` -> the real
origin); fixed to `same-origin`; pinned by a test that fails if it is ever
tightened back.

This is the exact shape ADR-2608136000 is about, in a place it had not been
looked for: not a check that could not run, but a check driven differently
by the test than by reality. Anyone else in this workspace who sets
`no-referrer` on a page that has a form and an Origin check will hit it.

Consequences

- `kagi ui` exists: Items (metadata only), Devices (with revoke), Vault
  (unlock envelope and identity). ADR-2607230100's GUI follow-up is closed
  for revocation; device GRANT stays on the CLI, because it is a two-machine
  ceremony with a fingerprint read aloud and a window on one of them does
  not help.
- kagi takes a runtime dependency on jp-go-digital-design-system. With no
  bundle, the design system IS the front end.
- New source is `.cljc` (ADR-2608201300). The JVM half is marked with a
  reader conditional rather than by file extension: request parsing is
  portable and unit-tested without a socket; the socket is not portable,
  because it has to live in the process that holds the JDK's PQC primitives.
- Verified: 222 tests / 811 assertions, 0 failures; clj-kondo 0 errors.
  Every guard was checked by breaking it and watching its own test fail —
  including one that did NOT discriminate at first (the view-reachability
  check found fragments in the routing CSS, so a view dropped from the nav
  was still 'found'; it reads the <nav> element alone now).
- Search and item detail landed after the first cut (kagi aecf5c23). Search
  is a GET over metadata only — no ciphertext is touched, so a query that
  could only match a secret's contents finds nothing, and a filter matching
  nothing is drawn differently from an empty vault. Opening one item is the
  first thing in the window that decrypts, so it happens only when the reader
  names one (`:detail` is not called while drawing a list, and a test fails
  if it ever is), under its own ledger purpose `:ui-detail`. What comes back
  is shape: `kagi.vault-read/strip-sensitive` drops concealed / totp /
  credit-card / ssh-key values and keeps the fields, so the screen says a
  password is SET rather than showing an empty cell. That work added
  `kagi.vault-read/read-one`, which answers :ok / :raw / :denied / :absent as
  four distinguishable things — `kagitaba-items` folds three of them into an
  empty result, which is right for a list and wrong for opening one item.
- Still not done, and named rather than left implicit: no `kagi fill` from
  the window, and no way to add or edit an item. Both are write or
  reveal-adjacent paths, and each deserves its own decision.")))
